AGENTIC AI FOR DEVOPS
& IT AUTOMATION
Build Your Own AI-Powered Incident Investigation Agent
From Python code to an intelligent infrastructure investigator. Build an agent that checks a real Kubernetes pod, gathers evidence, explains possible failures, and creates a professional incident report.
RHEL 10  |  k3d Kubernetes  |  Python  |  Claude API  |  LangChain
8 Phases  |  Practical, beginner-friendly build

What you will build
•	A Python application that accepts a pod name and namespace.
•	Diagnostic tools that read actual pod status, events, logs, labels, node placement, and resource usage.
•	A Claude-powered explanation that separates observed facts from likely causes.
•	A safety checkpoint before any restart action. The first version focuses on investigation and reporting.
•	A real Markdown report saved on disk with pod name, namespace, status, node, CPU usage, RAM usage, events, and recommendations.
•	Automated tests for report creation, missing pods, namespace handling, and safe error handling.
Simple project story
Imagine a developer says: “My application pod is not working.” Instead of manually running many commands, you run one Python program. The program checks Kubernetes, collects real evidence, asks Claude to explain that evidence, and saves a report. The agent does not invent CPU or RAM values: if Kubernetes metrics are unavailable, the report clearly says so.
Eight-phase plan
Phase	What we do	Result
1	Prepare RHEL 10, Python, Docker, k3d and kubectl	Local lab ready
2	Create a Kubernetes cluster and run a sample pod	Real pod running
3	Create the Python project and connect to Kubernetes	Python can inspect pods
4	Build simple diagnostic tools	Status, logs, events and metrics
5	Connect Claude API and LangChain	AI explains collected evidence
6	Build the investigation flow and safety rules	Controlled investigation
7	Test healthy and broken scenarios	Tests and troubleshooting
8	Run a pod and ask the reporting tool for a real report	Markdown report with CPU/RAM/name/namespace

 Before we start: understand the main words
What is an AI agent?
A normal chatbot mainly answers your question. An AI agent can follow a task, use tools, inspect results, and then produce an answer. In this project, Python controls the process and Claude helps explain the evidence.
What is a tool?
A tool is a normal Python function that the agent can use. Examples: get_pod_status(), get_pod_logs(), get_pod_events(), get_pod_metrics(), and create_report(). A tool must return real data or a clear error.
What is LangChain?
LangChain provides a standard way to describe Python functions as tools and connect them to language models. We keep the project intentionally small: a few readable functions instead of a large framework.
What is a real report?
The report is built from the Kubernetes API and kubectl metrics. Claude can explain the collected facts, but it must not invent missing measurements. CPU and RAM usage normally require Metrics Server. If metrics are not available, the report marks them as unavailable rather than showing made-up values.
Important lab notes
•	Use this lab on a test machine or local cluster, not a production cluster.
•	Do not commit the .env file or API key to Git.
•	The report tool is read-only. It does not delete or restart pods.
•	A successful install depends on internet access, Docker, package repositories, API access, and compatible versions. No guide can guarantee every machine is identical.
•	Use the same namespace consistently: ai-sre-lab.
PHASE 1 — Prepare RHEL 10
Goal: install the basic tools and confirm that Docker, kubectl, and k3d work.
Step 1.1 — Update the system
sudo dnf update -y
sudo dnf install -y curl git vim tar gzip
Step 1.2 — Install Python tools
sudo dnf install -y python3 python3-pip python3-devel gcc
Step 1.3 — Check Docker
If Docker is already installed, start and test it. If it is not installed, install Docker Engine using the official Docker instructions for RHEL-compatible systems, then return here.
sudo systemctl enable --now docker
sudo systemctl status docker --no-pager
docker version
If your user cannot run Docker, use sudo for Docker commands or add your user to the docker group and sign in again. Membership in the docker group grants powerful host access.
Step 1.4 — Install kubectl and k3d
Install the current stable kubectl binary and k3d from their official installation instructions. Then verify:
kubectl version --client
k3d version
docker ps
If these commands work, continue. If a command is missing, finish its official installation before moving on.
PHASE 2 — Create the cluster and run a pod
Goal: create a small local Kubernetes cluster and run NGINX as the application we will investigate.
Step 2.1 — Create the k3d cluster
k3d cluster create pod-lab --agents 1 --servers 1
Step 2.2 — Check the cluster
kubectl cluster-info
kubectl get nodes
Step 2.3 — Create the namespace
kubectl create namespace ai-sre-lab
If the namespace already exists, that is okay. You can check it with:
kubectl get namespace ai-sre-lab
Step 2.4 — Create a sample Deployment
Create the folder first:
mkdir -p ~/pod-investigation-agent/k8s
cd ~/pod-investigation-agent
Create the following file. A Deployment keeps the NGINX pod running if it exits.
Full file path: ~/pod-investigation-agent/k8s/demo.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: demo-web
  namespace: ai-sre-lab
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: demo-web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: demo-web
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx:stable
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: "50m"
              memory: "64Mi"
            limits:
              cpu: "250m"
              memory: "128Mi"
Apply the file and wait for the pod:
kubectl apply -f ~/pod-investigation-agent/k8s/demo.yaml
kubectl rollout status deployment/demo-web -n ai-sre-lab
kubectl get pods -n ai-sre-lab -o wide
Write down the pod name shown by the last command. It will usually look like demo-web- followed by a generated suffix.
