If the model name is not available to your account, replace it with a currently supported Claude model name available to you. API model availability can change.
PHASE 4 — Build the diagnostic tools
Goal: create simple Python functions that read real Kubernetes information. We use kubectl because it is already configured to talk to the local cluster.
Step 4.1 — Create pod_tools.py
This file runs read-only kubectl commands. It checks the pod, logs, events, and resource metrics. All commands use a fixed namespace supplied by configuration or an explicit argument.
Full file path: ~/pod-investigation-agent/pod_tools.py
import json
import os
import subprocess
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path
 
from dotenv import load_dotenv
 
load_dotenv()
 
DEFAULT_NAMESPACE = os.getenv("K8S_NAMESPACE", "ai-sre-lab")
 
 
def run_kubectl(args, timeout=20):
    """Run kubectl safely and return stdout or a readable error."""
    command = ["kubectl"] + list(args)
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            command, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout, check=False
        )
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return {"ok": False, "error": f"Command timed out: {' '.join(command)}"}
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return {"ok": False, "error": "kubectl was not found. Install kubectl first."}
 
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "error": result.stderr.strip() or result.stdout.strip()
        }
    return {"ok": True, "output": result.stdout.strip()}
 
 
def get_pod_status(pod_name, namespace=DEFAULT_NAMESPACE):
    result = run_kubectl([
        "get", "pod", pod_name, "-n", namespace, "-o", "json"
    ])
    if not result["ok"]:
        return result
 
    pod = json.loads(result["output"])
    statuses = pod.get("status", {}).get("containerStatuses", [])
    return {
        "ok": True,
        "name": pod["metadata"]["name"],
        "namespace": pod["metadata"]["namespace"],
        "phase": pod.get("status", {}).get("phase", "Unknown"),
        "node": pod.get("spec", {}).get("nodeName", "Not scheduled"),
        "pod_ip": pod.get("status", {}).get("podIP", "Not assigned"),
        "created": pod.get("metadata", {}).get("creationTimestamp", "Unknown"),
        "labels": pod.get("metadata", {}).get("labels", {}),
        "containers": [
            {
                "name": item.get("name", "unknown"),
                "ready": item.get("ready", False),
                "restart_count": item.get("restartCount", 0),
                "state": item.get("state", {})
            }
            for item in statuses
        ],
        "owner_references": pod.get("metadata", {}).get("ownerReferences", [])
    }
 
 
def get_pod_logs(pod_name, namespace=DEFAULT_NAMESPACE, tail=80):
    result = run_kubectl([
        "logs", pod_name, "-n", namespace, "--all-containers=true",
        f"--tail={int(tail)}"
    ])
    if not result["ok"]:
        return result
    return {"ok": True, "logs": result["output"] or "(No log lines returned)"}
 
 
def get_pod_events(pod_name, namespace=DEFAULT_NAMESPACE):
    result = run_kubectl([
        "get", "events", "-n", namespace,
        "--field-selector", f"involvedObject.name={pod_name}",
        "--sort-by=.lastTimestamp", "-o", "wide"
    ])
    if not result["ok"]:
        return result
    return {"ok": True, "events": result["output"] or "(No events returned)"}
 
 
def get_pod_metrics(pod_name, namespace=DEFAULT_NAMESPACE):
    """Read live CPU/RAM usage from Metrics Server via kubectl top."""
    result = run_kubectl([
        "top", "pod", pod_name, "-n", namespace, "--containers"
    ])
    if not result["ok"]:
        return {
            "ok": False,
            "cpu_ram": "Unavailable",
            "note": "kubectl top failed. Metrics Server may not be installed, "
                    "may not be ready, or the pod may not have metrics yet.",
            "error": result["error"]
        }
    return {"ok": True, "cpu_ram": result["output"]}
 
 
def get_pod_description(pod_name, namespace=DEFAULT_NAMESPACE):
    result = run_kubectl(["describe", "pod", pod_name, "-n", namespace])
    if not result["ok"]:
        return result
    return {"ok": True, "description": result["output"]}
 
 
def collect_pod_evidence(pod_name, namespace=DEFAULT_NAMESPACE):
    """Gather the evidence used by the AI and the report writer."""
    return {
        "collected_at_utc": datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(),
        "pod": get_pod_status(pod_name, namespace),
        "metrics": get_pod_metrics(pod_name, namespace),
        "events": get_pod_events(pod_name, namespace),
        "logs": get_pod_logs(pod_name, namespace),
        "description": get_pod_description(pod_name, namespace)
    }
Step 4.2 — Test the tools from the terminal
kubectl get pods -n ai-sre-lab
kubectl top pod -n ai-sre-lab
kubectl describe pod -n ai-sre-lab
The first command should show your pod. The second command needs Metrics Server. If `kubectl top` fails, continue with the Metrics Server step below; the report will clearly mark CPU/RAM as unavailable until metrics work.
Step 4.3 — Enable CPU/RAM metrics if needed
A fresh k3d cluster may not include Metrics Server. Install it using its official Kubernetes release manifest, then check its pod. The command below downloads a live upstream manifest, so run it only if your lab permits fetching official Kubernetes components.
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server/releases/latest/download/components.yaml
kubectl -n kube-system get deployment metrics-server
If Metrics Server cannot scrape the local k3d kubelet because of certificate verification, do not silently ignore the error. For a disposable local training cluster only, you can configure Metrics Server to use `--kubelet-insecure-tls`; this weakens kubelet certificate validation and must not be copied to production. After the change, wait for the deployment and test `kubectl top pod -n ai-sre-lab` again.
kubectl -n kube-system rollout status deployment/metrics-server --timeout=120s
kubectl top pod -n ai-sre-lab
PHASE 5 — Connect Claude and LangChain
Goal: send collected evidence to Claude for an explanation. We do not ask Claude to guess values; we provide the real tool output.
Step 5.1 — Create claude_client.py
Full file path: ~/pod-investigation-agent/claude_client.py
import json
import os
 
from anthropic import Anthropic
from dotenv import load_dotenv
 
load_dotenv()
 
 
def explain_incident(evidence):
    api_key = os.getenv("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY")
    model = os.getenv("ANTHROPIC_MODEL")
 
    if not api_key or api_key == "replace_with_your_real_key":
        return (
            "AI explanation unavailable: set a valid ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in .env. "
            "The collected Kubernetes evidence is still included in the report."
        )
    if not model:
        return "AI explanation unavailable: set ANTHROPIC_MODEL in .env."
 
    prompt = f"""
You are a careful DevOps incident investigator.
Use only the evidence below. Do not invent CPU, RAM, timestamps, or events.
Separate confirmed observations from hypotheses.
If CPU/RAM metrics are unavailable, say so.
Give a short incident summary, likely cause, evidence, recommended next checks,
and a confidence level. Do not claim you changed the cluster.
 
EVIDENCE JSON:
{json.dumps(evidence, indent=2)[:18000]}
"""
    try:
        client = Anthropic(api_key=api_key)
        response = client.messages.create(
            model=model,
            max_tokens=900,
            messages=[{"role": "user", "content": prompt}]
        )
        pieces = []
        for block in response.content:
            if getattr(block, "type", None) == "text":
                pieces.append(block.text)
        return "\n".join(pieces).strip() or "Claude returned no text."
    except Exception as exc:
        return f"AI explanation unavailable: {type(exc).__name__}: {exc}"
Step 5.2 — Create LangChain tools
LangChain tools make Python functions available in a consistent tool format. This beginner version uses the tools directly from Python, so the workflow remains easy to understand and test.
Full file path: ~/pod-investigation-agent/agent_tools.py
from langchain_core.tools import tool
from dotenv import load_dotenv
 
from pod_tools import (
    get_pod_status,
    get_pod_logs,
    get_pod_events,
    get_pod_metrics,
)
 
load_dotenv()
 
 
@tool
def inspect_pod(pod_name: str, namespace: str = "ai-sre-lab") -> dict:
    """Read the current status, node, labels and container state of one pod."""
    return get_pod_status(pod_name, namespace)
 
 
@tool
def read_pod_logs(pod_name: str, namespace: str = "ai-sre-lab") -> dict:
    """Read the latest log lines from all containers in one pod."""
    return get_pod_logs(pod_name, namespace)
 
 
@tool
def read_pod_events(pod_name: str, namespace: str = "ai-sre-lab") -> dict:
    """Read Kubernetes events related to one pod."""
    return get_pod_events(pod_name, namespace)
 
 
@tool
def read_pod_cpu_ram(pod_name: str, namespace: str = "ai-sre-lab") -> dict:
    """Read live pod/container CPU and memory usage using kubectl top."""
    return get_pod_metrics(pod_name, namespace)
In simple terms: `@tool` describes what a function does. We call these functions from our controlled Python workflow. This keeps the first version readable and avoids giving the model direct shell access.
PHASE 6 — Build the investigation and report workflow
Step 6.1 — Create reporting.py
This creates a Markdown file using the actual evidence. CPU/RAM are copied from `kubectl top`; if metrics are missing, the report states that clearly.
Full file path: ~/pod-investigation-agent/reporting.py
import json
import os
import re
from datetime import datetime
from pathlib import Path
 
from dotenv import load_dotenv
 
load_dotenv()
 
 
def safe_filename(value):
    return re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z0-9_.-]+", "_", value)
 
 
def create_markdown_report(pod_name, namespace, evidence, ai_explanation):
    report_dir = Path(os.getenv("REPORT_DIR", "reports"))
    report_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
 
    pod = evidence.get("pod", {})
    metrics = evidence.get("metrics", {})
    events = evidence.get("events", {})
    logs = evidence.get("logs", {})
    description = evidence.get("description", {})
 
    timestamp = datetime.now().strftime("%Y%m%d_%H%M%S")
    file_path = report_dir / f"incident_{safe_filename(namespace)}_{safe_filename(pod_name)}_{timestamp}.md"
 
    cpu_ram = metrics.get("cpu_ram", "Unavailable") if metrics.get("ok") else "Unavailable"
    metrics_note = metrics.get("note", "")
    if not metrics.get("ok"):
        metrics_note += " " + metrics.get("error", "")
 
    content = f"""# Kubernetes Pod Incident Report
 
- **Generated at:** {datetime.now().isoformat(timespec="seconds")}
- **Pod name:** {pod_name}
- **Namespace:** {namespace}
- **Status:** {pod.get("phase", "Unknown")}
- **Node:** {pod.get("node", "Unknown")}
- **Pod IP:** {pod.get("pod_ip", "Unknown")}
- **Created:** {pod.get("created", "Unknown")}
 
## CPU and RAM usage
 
```text
{cpu_ram}
```
 
{metrics_note}
 
> CPU/RAM above is live usage from Kubernetes Metrics Server when available. It is not the pod's configured limit. If marked unavailable, no usage number was invented.
 
## Container state
 
```json
{json.dumps(pod.get("containers", []), indent=2)}
```
 
## Kubernetes events
 
```text
{events.get("events", events.get("error", "Unavailable"))}
```
 
## Recent logs
 
```text
{logs.get("logs", logs.get("error", "Unavailable"))}
```
 
## Pod description
 
```text
{description.get("description", description.get("error", "Unavailable"))[:8000]}
```
 
## AI investigation
 
{ai_explanation}
 
## Verification and limitations
 
- This report is based on evidence collected from the current Kubernetes cluster.
- The AI explanation is advisory; verify recommendations before making changes.
- This program did not restart, delete, or modify the pod.
- Missing metrics or unavailable logs are reported as unavailable, not guessed.
"""
    file_path.write_text(content, encoding="utf-8")
    return str(file_path)
Step 6.2 — Create main.py
This is the main entry point. It validates the pod name and namespace, calls the diagnostic tools, asks Claude to explain the evidence, and saves the report.
Full file path: ~/pod-investigation-agent/main.py
import argparse
import json
import re
 
from dotenv import load_dotenv
 
from pod_tools import collect_pod_evidence
from claude_client import explain_incident
from reporting import create_markdown_report
from agent_tools import inspect_pod, read_pod_logs, read_pod_events, read_pod_cpu_ram
 
load_dotenv()
 
NAME_PATTERN = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9]([-a-z0-9.]*[a-z0-9])?$")
 
 
def validate_name(value, label):
    if not value or len(value) > 253 or not NAME_PATTERN.fullmatch(value):
        raise ValueError(f"Invalid {label}: {value!r}")
    return value
 
 
def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Investigate a Kubernetes pod and create a Markdown report."
    )
    parser.add_argument("--pod", required=True, help="Exact pod name")
    parser.add_argument("--namespace", default="ai-sre-lab", help="Kubernetes namespace")
    args = parser.parse_args()
 
    pod_name = validate_name(args.pod, "pod name")
    namespace = validate_name(args.namespace, "namespace")
 
    print("\n[1/5] Checking pod status with LangChain tool...")
    status = inspect_pod.invoke({"pod_name": pod_name, "namespace": namespace})
    if not status.get("ok"):
        print("Could not inspect pod:", status.get("error", status))
        print("Check the pod name and namespace using: kubectl get pods -n", namespace)
        raise SystemExit(1)
    print(json.dumps(status, indent=2))
 
    print("\n[2/5] Reading logs with LangChain tool...")
    print(read_pod_logs.invoke({"pod_name": pod_name, "namespace": namespace}))
 
    print("\n[3/5] Reading events with LangChain tool...")
    print(read_pod_events.invoke({"pod_name": pod_name, "namespace": namespace}))
 
    print("\n[4/5] Reading CPU/RAM with LangChain tool...")
    print(read_pod_cpu_ram.invoke({"pod_name": pod_name, "namespace": namespace}))
 
    print("\n[5/5] Collecting full evidence and generating the report...")
    evidence = collect_pod_evidence(pod_name, namespace)
    explanation = explain_incident(evidence)
    report_path = create_markdown_report(
        pod_name, namespace, evidence, explanation
    )
 
    print("\nReport created successfully:")
    print(report_path)
    print("\nOpen it with:")
    print(f"cat {report_path}")
 
 
if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
Safety note: this version is intentionally read-only. It investigates and reports, but does not automatically restart or delete a pod. This is a good first production-style safety boundary.
PHASE 7 — Test the project
Goal: test both normal and error situations before the final live report demo.
Step 7.1 — Create test_pod_tools.py
These tests check that command construction is safe and that the report can be generated from supplied evidence. They do not require a live Kubernetes cluster.
Full file path: ~/pod-investigation-agent/tests/test_pod_tools.py
from pod_tools import get_pod_metrics
from reporting import create_markdown_report
 
 
def test_metrics_error_is_reported(monkeypatch):
    import pod_tools
 
    monkeypatch.setattr(
        pod_tools,
        "run_kubectl",
        lambda args, timeout=20: {"ok": False, "error": "metrics unavailable"}
    )
    result = get_pod_metrics("demo-web-abc", "ai-sre-lab")
    assert result["ok"] is False
    assert result["cpu_ram"] == "Unavailable"
    assert "metrics unavailable" in result["error"]
 
 
def test_report_contains_pod_identity_and_metrics(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
    import reporting
 
    monkeypatch.setenv("REPORT_DIR", str(tmp_path))
    evidence = {
        "pod": {
            "phase": "Running",
            "node": "k3d-pod-lab-agent-0",
            "pod_ip": "10.42.0.10",
            "created": "2026-10-30T00:00:00Z",
            "containers": [{"name": "nginx", "ready": True, "restart_count": 0}]
        },
        "metrics": {"ok": True, "cpu_ram": "nginx 2m 12Mi"},
        "events": {"ok": True, "events": "No events"},
        "logs": {"ok": True, "logs": "nginx started"},
        "description": {"ok": True, "description": "Pod description"}
    }
    path = create_markdown_report(
        "demo-web-abc", "ai-sre-lab", evidence, "No issue observed."
    )
    content = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
    assert "demo-web-abc" in content
    assert "ai-sre-lab" in content
    assert "nginx 2m 12Mi" in content
    assert "No issue observed." in content
 
 
def test_report_marks_missing_metrics(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
    monkeypatch.setenv("REPORT_DIR", str(tmp_path))
    evidence = {
        "pod": {"phase": "Running", "node": "node-1"},
        "metrics": {"ok": False, "cpu_ram": "Unavailable", "note": "Metrics Server missing"},
        "events": {"events": "Unavailable"},
        "logs": {"logs": "Unavailable"},
        "description": {"description": "Unavailable"}
    }
    path = create_markdown_report(
        "demo-web-abc", "ai-sre-lab", evidence, "Metrics not available."
    )
    content = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
    assert "Unavailable" in content
    assert "Metrics Server missing" in content
Step 7.2 — Run the automated tests
cd ~/pod-investigation-agent
source .venv/bin/activate
pytest -q
Expected result: three tests pass. If a test fails, read the first traceback line that points to your project file, fix that issue, and run pytest again.
Step 7.3 — Test a missing pod
python main.py --pod pod-that-does-not-exist --namespace ai-sre-lab
Expected result: the program prints a clear Kubernetes error and exits. It should not create a misleading report for a pod that does not exist.
Step 7.4 — Create a controlled broken pod
This test creates a pod that cannot download its image. It is useful for observing ImagePullBackOff. It is safe for the lab because it does not alter the healthy Deployment.
Full file path: ~/pod-investigation-agent/k8s/broken-pod.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: broken-image-demo
  namespace: ai-sre-lab
  labels:
    app: broken-image-demo
spec:
  containers:
    - name: web
      image: nginx:tag-that-does-not-exist
kubectl apply -f ~/pod-investigation-agent/k8s/broken-pod.yaml
kubectl get pod broken-image-demo -n ai-sre-lab -w
Wait until the status becomes ErrImagePull or ImagePullBackOff, then press Ctrl+C to stop watching. Run the agent against the broken pod in Phase 8. It should collect events that point to the image-pull problem.
Troubleshooting checklist
•	kubectl cannot connect: run `kubectl get nodes` and check `k3d cluster list`.
•	Pod name not found: run `kubectl get pods -n ai-sre-lab` and copy the exact name.
•	Logs unavailable: check whether the container has started; a failed image pull often has events but no application logs.
•	`kubectl top` fails: check Metrics Server deployment and wait for metrics to become available.
•	Claude API fails: verify the key, account access, model name, network access, and API quota. The report should still be created with an AI-unavailable note.
•	Package import fails: activate `.venv` and run `pip install -r requirements.txt` again.
PHASE 8 — Run a pod and generate a real incident report
This is the final practical. You will run the tool against a real pod in your k3d cluster. The report includes the exact pod name, namespace, status, node, CPU usage and RAM usage when Metrics Server is available, plus logs, events, and Claude's evidence-based explanation.
Step 8.1 — Make sure a pod is running
kubectl get pods -n ai-sre-lab -o wide
kubectl rollout status deployment/demo-web -n ai-sre-lab
Copy the exact pod name from the output. Example names change every time, so do not blindly copy the example name.
Step 8.2 — Run the reporting agent for the healthy pod
Replace `<REAL_POD_NAME>` with the pod name you copied. Keep the namespace as `ai-sre-lab`.
cd ~/pod-investigation-agent
source .venv/bin/activate
python main.py --pod <REAL_POD_NAME> --namespace ai-sre-lab
The terminal will show five stages: pod inspection, logs, events, CPU/RAM metrics, and report creation. The final line prints the path to the generated Markdown report.
Step 8.3 — Open and inspect the report
ls -lh ~/pod-investigation-agent/reports
cat ~/pod-investigation-agent/reports/incident_ai-sre-lab_<POD_NAME>_<TIMESTAMP>.md
Use the exact filename printed by the program rather than typing the placeholder literally. The report should contain:
•	Pod name and namespace.
•	Current pod phase, node, pod IP, creation time, and container readiness/restart counts.
•	Live CPU and memory usage from `kubectl top`, if available.
•	Recent pod events and logs.
•	An AI investigation with observations, likely cause, recommendations, and uncertainty.
•	A note confirming that the tool did not change the cluster.
Step 8.4 — Generate a report for the broken pod
python main.py --pod broken-image-demo --namespace ai-sre-lab
Expected finding: the pod is likely Pending and/or in ImagePullBackOff because the requested image tag does not exist. The event output should support this explanation. CPU/RAM may be unavailable because the container never started. The agent should say that rather than inventing metrics.
Step 8.5 — Compare the two reports
Check	Healthy pod	Broken image pod
Pod name / namespace	Actual Deployment pod / ai-sre-lab	broken-image-demo / ai-sre-lab
Status	Usually Running	Usually Pending / image-pull failure
CPU and RAM	Usually available after metrics start	May be unavailable if container never starts
Evidence	Container state, logs, events	Image pull events and pod description
AI conclusion	Report current observations	Likely image tag/pull problem, backed by events
Step 8.6 — Clean up the broken test pod
kubectl delete pod broken-image-demo -n ai-sre-lab
This deletes only the deliberately broken test pod. It does not delete the healthy Deployment.
Final project checklist
•	RHEL 10 lab and k3d cluster are working.
•	The healthy NGINX Deployment is running.
•	Python virtual environment and packages are installed.
•	Diagnostic tools can read pod status, logs, events, and metrics.
•	Claude explanation works, or the report clearly records why AI was unavailable.
•	Automated tests pass.
•	A report for a healthy pod is saved.
•	A report for a broken-image pod is saved.
•	Each report includes pod name and namespace and either real CPU/RAM usage or an honest unavailable message.
•	No automatic restart, deletion, or other remediation occurs.
Final deliverables
•	Python source files: pod_tools.py, agent_tools.py, claude_client.py, reporting.py, main.py
•	Kubernetes YAML files: demo.yaml and broken-pod.yaml
•	Configuration template in .env (keep the real API key private)
•	Automated test file and test results
•	Markdown RCA reports in the reports/ folder
•	A repeatable command to investigate any pod in the selected namespace
One-command reminder
After you know the pod name, the final command is:
python main.py --pod <REAL_POD_NAME> --namespace ai-sre-lab
Remember: the AI is an assistant to the investigation, not a replacement for evidence. Kubernetes supplies the facts; the program records them; Claude explains them; you decide what to do next.

